#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315a3560-94c3-5a3d-8a88-b6b6b093ae82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358938} {:answer/id #uuid "6d98f302-d61f-5d4f-b443-174e9ac52b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358939} {:answer/id #uuid "161d95cf-2985-5e9f-9993-fbe1089d2210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358940} {:answer/id #uuid "d073bbc1-76bd-5baf-9bad-ad3e51fa4c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358941} {:answer/id #uuid "53de5759-f0e6-5089-8fd8-62f47a4b4c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315a3560-94c3-5a3d-8a88-b6b6b093ae82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358938} :answer-field/id #uuid "f79d7e0b-2b15-5fa9-9960-242560c81a1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e47187ec-f899-5cff-85d8-51e83161b9ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-125075" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The time it takes for a student to complete math homework\n- The number of hours you spend jogging in a week\n- The number of candies in a jar" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
